WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.860
Obviously, we have to talk about gun control
this week.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.300
Over the course of our many Rubin Report interviews,
I've sat across from an array people who are

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.439
pro-gun rights, anti-gun altogether, or somewhere
in between.

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:21.090
While the events surrounding the gun conversation
are always horrific...with this week's tragic

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:26.110
events in Vegas being perhaps the worst of
them all...I believe the conversation around

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:31.320
guns, when done right, can offer powerful
insight into how people think about society

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.340
at large.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.150
Gun ownership and the right to bear arms brings
up issues about the American constitution,

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:44.210
states rights, personal responsibility, mental
health, radicalization...both religious and

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:45.330
otherwise...and much more.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:50.420
Unfortunately, as is the case so often these
days, we're seemingly caught between two groups:

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.370
the people who want to ban guns completely,
and the people who don't want to have any

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:58.390
sort of conversation about sensible gun laws.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:04.150
While I absolutely support the 2nd Amendment,
at the same time I absolutely acknowledge

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:10.240
the United States has what seems to be a unique
problem related to mass gun violence.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.200
Lost in the midst of the awful events of Last
Vegas this week are the other senseless shootings

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.889
across the country, like the 5 people killed
and 30 others wounded by gun fire in Chicago

00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:22.289
this past weekend.

00:01:22.289 --> 00:01:26.450
The weekend before that, 3 were killed and
36 wounded in Chicago, and the weekend before

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:31.299
that it was 11 dead and 29 wounded in the
very same city.

00:01:31.299 --> 00:01:35.670
We've included a link down below which tracks
the gun related violence across Chicago occurring

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:39.249
every single weekend, and it's disturbing
to say the least.

00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:44.759
I bring up Chicago not to deflect from the
events in Las Vegas, but to show this murderous

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:50.819
violence is happening in one of our biggest
cities literally every single day, happening

00:01:50.819 --> 00:01:55.060
so often at this point the mainstream media
has all but given up on reporting on it.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:59.219
The reasons behind how and why people murder
others also makes it hard to find one law

00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:01.799
around guns that works in every situation.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:07.359
There no doubt is a difference between a murder
that occurs during a robbery v. gang violence

00:02:07.359 --> 00:02:13.470
v. terrorism v. suicides v. a random shooter
with no known political or religious motive.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:18.070
And the same time, there are the times when
someone with a gun saves innocent lives during

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:22.830
a shoot-out, or when someone defends their
property and family by having a gun in the

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:23.830
home.

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:28.510
So again, while I support the 2nd Amendment,
I also recognize that we undoubtably have

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.090
a gun-related violence problem on our hands...just
look at the numbers.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:37.560
I can also acknowledge that guns in and of
themselves aren't the only problem here.

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:42.180
Without question, much of the carnage caused
by guns is due to people with mental health

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.650
issues gaining access to guns.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:49.130
The truth is that if I, or most the gun owners
in America, had access to the most deadly

00:02:49.130 --> 00:02:55.110
weapons on Earth, we wouldn't randomly use
those weapons against innocent people.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:59.810
Not only are mental health issues an aspect
to the people who commit mass shootings, but

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:06.220
also there is also a problem with any ideology
which drives people to commit these heinous

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:12.020
acts, be it a religious ideology, a political
ideology, or any other system of principals

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.690
or beliefs which could drive someone to kill.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:21.110
A weapon in and of itself can't kill anyone,
it takes a human being and a corrupt thought

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:24.850
process to pull the trigger and kill innocent
people.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:30.230
Also, as I'm recording this, it is still unclear
exactly what weapons were used in Vegas, but

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:35.231
authorities report finding about 20 other
fire arms in the shooter's hotel room, and

00:03:35.231 --> 00:03:40.790
it's possible that this man was using a military
style fully automatic rifle to commit this

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:42.040
heinous act.

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:49.150
For all the defense I've offered the 2nd Amendment,
I CAN NOT see how access to such weapons,

00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:53.910
which have the ability to mow down civilians
at an incredible rate and which are designed

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:57.960
for the battlefield, should be in the hands
of regular citizens.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.490
As we post this video the story about the
Vegas gunman is largely incomplete.

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:05.080
At the moment we don't seem to know his motives,
so it's hard to say if this was an act of

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.050
terrorism under the most specific definition
of the term, which includes having a political

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.740
motivation to kill.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:15.990
Nevada state’s defintion is a little more
broad, not including the need for a political

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:21.060
motivation, which would in fact classify this
killer as a Terrorist.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:26.760
Regardless of how we want to define this specific
act of horrific violence, or how the gun discussion

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.870
relates to terrorism, or the legitimate right
of every citizen to have the ability defend

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:37.370
themselves, we must get better about talking
about gun control between the shootings in

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:42.000
order to prevent more shootings, and not just
when these acts occur.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:46.820
Passing more laws in the heat of emotion is
rarely the right thing to do, even if they

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:52.150
are well intentioned, while doing nothing
and hoping these events will simply end is

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:58.970
just as misguided as hoping terrorism will
completely stop if we just ignore it.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:03.480
While we wait to find out more about the Vegas
shooter himself and at the same time mourn

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.760
the victims of this evil act, we should also
continue to talk to people on both sides of

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.570
the gun debate to try to come to sensible
place of agreement.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:17.660
I'm going to do my best to do just that -- if
you know of some interesting voices in this

00:05:17.660 --> 00:05:21.960
debate you'd like to hear on The Rubin Report,
let us know in the comment section right down

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:21.961
below.

